--TEST--
`Core\Request\Part::saveTo` takes `Core\IO::writeStream`'s own `{max?, overwrite?}` and nothing else, so a size written as text and an option the bag does not declare are both refused — `rule:core-classes/io-write-stream`, `rule:core-api/shape-rules` R2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The delegation is visible in the bag: `saveTo` names `writeStream`'s options
// rather than declaring a pair of its own, so the two cannot drift and there is
// one place `overwrite` defaults to `false`.
//
// Asked at both of its edges, as `readAll`'s is. `rule:http-server/a-part-is-consumed-in-one-of-three-ways` writes a bound in
// prose as `max: "50M"`; the argument is a count of bytes, because a size in
// text is a second parser in front of a number. And the bag is closed: a
// misspelt option is refused rather than ignored, which is what keeps a program
// from believing it raised a ceiling it left at the default.
//
// `temp:` is the one worth naming. PHP's upload had a temporary file and this
// member is the reason there is none -- so the option a reader might reach for
// out of habit is not merely undeclared, it is the thing `rule:http-server/an-upload-is-received-only-through-files` removed.
foreach (Core\Request::files() as Core\Request\Part $part) {
    $part->saveTo("/tmp/report.pdf", {max: "50M"});
    $part->saveTo("/tmp/report.pdf", {temp: true});
}
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0401]: expected `uint`, found `string`
   --> case.nvs:16:44
%A
error: aborting due to 2 errors
